    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the traveler's final position relative to the starting point, we need to calculate the net effect of each leg of the journey on the traveler's north-south and east-west coordinates.\n\n1. **First Leg: 18 miles north**\n   - The traveler moves 18 miles north, so the north coordinate increases by 18 miles.\n\n2. **Second Leg: 11 miles west**\n   - The traveler moves 11 miles west, so the west coordinate decreases by 11 miles.\n\n3. **Third Leg: 6 miles south**\n   - The traveler moves 6 miles south, so the south coordinate decreases by 6 miles.\n\n4. **Fourth Leg: 6 miles east**\n   - The traveler moves 6 miles east, so the east coordinate increases by 6 miles.\n\nNow, we sum up the net changes in each direction:\n- Net change in north-south direction: \\(18 - 6 = 12\\) miles north.\n- Net change in east-west direction: \\(6 - 11 = -5\\) miles west (or 5 miles east).\n\nThe traveler's final position is 12 miles north and 5 miles west of the starting point. To find the straight-line distance from the starting point to the final position, we use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(12)^2 + (5)^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nThus, the traveler is \\(\\boxed{13}\\) miles from the starting point.",
